 \section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}
 \textbf{Leakage Testing:}\label{sec:validating-speculative-execution-defenses}
-Prior work validates speculative-execution security at three stages of a design: it tests fabricated CPUs, verifies or fuzzes RTL, and, before RTL, model-checks hand-written models of early-stage designs~\cite{pensieve} or tests simulated Spectre defenses. Revizor~\cite{revizor} and its follow-ups~\cite{oleksenko2023hideseekspectresefficient,hofmann2023speculationatfault} fuzz commercial CPUs against hardware-software contracts (\S\ref{sec:bg-contracts}), and Trevex~\cite{weber2026trevex} detects data-flow transient-execution vulnerabilities. \cutrep{In RTL, tools search for transient-execution vulnerabilities through differential execution}{In RTL, tools find transient-execution vulnerabilities by differential execution}~\cite{hur2022specdoctor,xu2025dejavuzz}, gadget-driven simulation~\cite{ghaniyoun2021introspectre}, information-flow tracking~\cite{rostami2024specure}, or contract coverage~\cite{geier2025coverage}. Scam-V~\cite{nemati2020scamv} validates side-channel models against hardware, and software tools test programs for Spectre gadgets~\cite{oleksenko2020specfuzz} or against leakage models~\cite{barthe2024lmtest}. \cutrep{In gem5, SpecCheck~\cite{mckevitt2023speccheck} identifies vulnerable transient execution in a running program.}{In gem5, SpecCheck~\cite{mckevitt2023speccheck} identifies vulnerable transient execution at run time.}
+Prior work validates speculative-execution security at three stages of a design: it tests fabricated CPUs, verifies or fuzzes RTL, and, before RTL, model-checks hand-written models of early-stage designs~\cite{pensieve} or tests simulated Spectre defenses. Revizor~\cite{revizor} and its follow-ups~\cite{oleksenko2023hideseekspectresefficient,hofmann2023speculationatfault} fuzz commercial CPUs against hardware-software contracts (\S\ref{sec:bg-contracts}), and Trevex~\cite{weber2026trevex} detects data-flow transient-execution vulnerabilities. \cutrep{In RTL, tools search for transient-execution vulnerabilities through differential execution}{In RTL, tools find transient-execution vulnerabilities by differential execution}~\cite{hur2022specdoctor,xu2025dejavuzz}, gadget-driven simulation~\cite{ghaniyoun2021introspectre}, information-flow tracking~\cite{rostami2024specure}, or contract coverage~\cite{geier2025coverage}. \cutsug{Scam-V~\cite{nemati2020scamv} validates side-channel models against hardware, and software tools test programs for Spectre gadgets~\cite{oleksenko2020specfuzz} or against leakage models~\cite{barthe2024lmtest}. }\cutrep{In gem5, SpecCheck~\cite{mckevitt2023speccheck} identifies vulnerable transient execution in a running program.}{In gem5, SpecCheck~\cite{mckevitt2023speccheck} identifies vulnerable transient execution at run time.}
 
 The closest prior work is AMuLeT~\cite{amulet} (\S\S\ref{sec:known-bugs} and~\ref{sec:comparison-prior-work}), which fuzzes simulated Spectre defenses against the hardware-software contracts \contract{CT-SEQ} and \contract{ARCH-SEQ}~\cite[\S 3.1]{amulet}\cutsug{; four of its nine reports fall outside the defenses' security guarantees}. Protean's fuzzer extends AMuLeT with an adversary model that observes the cycle at which each instruction reaches each pipeline stage~\cite[\S VII-B1]{protean}. AMuLeT varies mispredictions by keeping predictor state across test cases~\cite[\S 3.2]{amulet} and root-causes each contract violation manually, which took hours to days per violation~\cite[\S 3.3]{amulet}. In contrast, \toolname{} checks one run against the defense's security guarantee, forces each test's mispredictions (\S\ref{sec:speculation-directives}), and synthesizes each test from an execution graph that identifies the transmitter, the protected data, and the speculation primitive responsible (\S\ref{sec:leakage-predicate}), so only locating the responsible code in the defense is manual.
 
